  <h2>Brief Biographical Statement</h2>
  <p>
    I am a researcher in legal psychology working on how the criminal justice
    system handles welfare-oriented information about defendants — their
    developmental backgrounds, disabilities, and social isolation. My core
    question is whether and how such information shapes sentencing decisions
    made by both lay citizens and professional judges, and what its proper
    role might be in a system where criminal justice and social welfare meet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bridging law and social psychology, I take a mixed-methods approach:
    combining vignette experiments, path
    analysis, natural language processing of sentencing texts, and
    explainable machine learning. Through this work I aim to clarify how
    Japanese criminal justice can productively engage with welfare
    considerations in sentencing. Throughout, I try to pair a broad interest
    in people and society with warmth toward those involved and a commitment
    to calm, evidence-based discussion.
  </p>
